function openAimEl101(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;if(!aimElPrerequisiteMet()){p.innerHTML='<div class="acaResult fail"><h3>AIM-CA™ PREREQUISITE REQUIRED</h3></div>';return}
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">AIM-EL™ · AEL-101</span><h2>Executive AI Authority & Accountability</h2><p>LEARN → EXECUTIVE CASE → KEY INSIGHT → LEADERSHIP DECISION → ASSESSMENT</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimElPath()">AIM-EL PATH</button></div>
 <article class="aimCaLesson"><h3>Course Overview</h3><p>Enterprise leaders do not need to reproduce every technical decision made by an AI system. They do need to understand, approve, and remain accountable for the authority their organizations place behind that intelligence. AEL-101 establishes the executive governance layer of AIM™.</p>
 <h3>Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Separate AI capability from enterprise authority.</li><li>Define accountable human ownership for consequential machine action.</li><li>Establish executive decision rights and human-reserved powers.</li><li>Recognize authority concentration across an AI workforce.</li><li>Connect Last Command to executive governance and continuity.</li></ul>
 <h3>1. Capability Is Not Authority</h3><p>A capable model may analyze, recommend, plan, negotiate, transact, modify systems, or coordinate other agents. None of those capabilities creates permission. AIM™ treats authority as a human-governed production state. The enterprise decides what the system may cause, under what conditions, for how long, and with what revocation path.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT</b><p>The model determines what an AI can reason about. AIM™ determines what it is allowed to cause.</p></div>
 <h3>2. Executive Accountability Does Not Automate Away</h3><p>Delegating execution does not eliminate human accountability. Every consequential AI authority should have an identifiable accountable owner with the organizational power to approve, constrain, suspend, and reassess it. The executive question is therefore not merely “Is the model accurate?” but “Who authorized this authority, who owns its consequences, and who can take it back?”</p>
 <h3>3. Decision Rights and Human-Reserved Powers</h3><p>Executives should explicitly reserve decisions whose legal, financial, safety, ethical, strategic, or public consequences require human judgment. Examples can include material policy exceptions, high-value financial commitments, contract signature, termination decisions, irreversible physical actions, and expansion of machine authority. These reservations should be reflected in enforceable A/G/H/X boundaries rather than policy language alone.</p>
 <h3>4. Authority Concentration Is a Portfolio Risk</h3><p>Individual agents may appear bounded while the enterprise AI workforce accumulates substantial combined reach across finance, identity, infrastructure, data, customers, and physical operations. Leadership therefore needs portfolio visibility into cumulative authority, shared dependencies, common control points, delegation chains, and correlated failure paths.</p>
 <h3>5. Executive Last Command</h3><p>At executive level, Last Command is a governance requirement as well as a technical mechanism. The organization must preserve externally enforceable revocation, cessation verification, safe reversion, accountable emergency authority, and a disciplined path to reauthorization.</p>
 <div class="caCase"><h3>Executive Case — Global Procurement AI Workforce</h3><p>A global company proposes an AI procurement workforce with access to ERP, vendor records, contract repositories, and payment-preparation systems. Business leaders expect $18 million in annual savings and want broad autonomy to accelerate adoption. Individually, several agents have modest transaction limits. Collectively, however, the workforce can initiate thousands of actions per day and relies on a shared identity-control service.</p><p><b>Executive issue:</b> the business case may be attractive while enterprise authority concentration is still unacceptable. Leadership must define ownership, aggregate ceilings, human-reserved powers, shared-control dependencies, escalation, and Last Command before approving production authority.</p></div>
 <div class="caPracticalSection"><span class="academyEyebrow">LEADERSHIP DECISION</span><h3>Executive Authority Charter</h3><p>Before assessment, write the five decisions you would require from the executive team: accountable owner, material authority ceiling, human-reserved powers, escalation/exception authority, and enterprise revocation owner.</p><textarea id="ael101Decision" rows="7" placeholder="Draft the executive authority charter decisions..."></textarea></div>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>EXECUTIVE PRINCIPLE</b><p>Executives do not need to understand every model decision. They must understand—and remain accountable for—the authority the enterprise gives the model.</p></div>
 <h3>AEL-101 Assessment</h3><p>10 questions · 80% required to earn the AEL-101 badge.</p>${AEL101_QUESTIONS.map((q,i)=>`<div class="acaQ"><b>${i+1}. ${q.q}</b>${q.a.map((a,j)=>`<label><input type="radio" name="ael101_${i}" value="${j}" onchange="ael101State.answers[${i}]=${j}"><span>${a}</span></label>`).join("")}</div>`).join("")}<button class="primary" onclick="submitAimEl101()">SUBMIT AEL-101 ASSESSMENT</button><div id="ael101Result"></div></article>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
