{q:"During a crisis, a business leader wants to restore an agent because every hour offline costs $600,000. What is the strongest AIM™ response?",a:["Restore immediately because financial loss overrides controls","Use a defined safe-reversion and reauthorization process based on evidence; urgency does not itself restore machine authority","Let the agent decide","Delete the audit trail"],c:1},
{q:"What is executive crisis command primarily designed to prevent?",a:["All business interruption","Confusion over authority, delayed containment, uncontrolled machine action, and premature restoration during a high-consequence event","Any employee discussion","Board oversight"],c:1},
{q:"Which communication principle is strongest during an AI authority incident?",a:["Only technical details matter","Maintain a common operating picture: known facts, uncertain facts, authority status, actions taken, owners, consequences, and next decision points","Avoid documenting uncertainty","Let every team use its own incident definition"],c:1},
{q:"What should happen to emergency human overrides after the crisis?",a:["Remain permanently active","Expire or be explicitly reassessed, documented, and reauthorized so emergency authority does not become normal authority","Transfer automatically to AI","Be hidden from audit"],c:1},
{q:"What is the executive standard for return to service?",a:["The agent responds to a test prompt","The enterprise has evidence of containment, root/control understanding appropriate to the decision, safe reversion, validated controls, accountable approval, and bounded reauthorization","The vendor says the outage is over","Revenue pressure increases"],c:1}
