function openAimCa109(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;aca109Answers={};
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">LEVEL 2 · ACA-109</span><h2>High-Consequence & Physical Autonomy Architecture</h2><p>Govern AI where machine decisions can affect health, safety, industrial equipment, infrastructure, and other high-consequence real-world systems.</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimCaPath()">← ALL AIM-CA™ MODULES</button></div>
 <div class="aimCaLesson">
 <div class="caLessonBand">LEARN → ARCHITECTURE EXAMPLE → KEY INSIGHT → DESIGN PRACTICE → ASSESSMENT → PASS ≥80% → EARN BADGE</div>
 <h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Separate optimization authority from non-negotiable safety authority.</li><li>Engineer deterministic safety invariants outside probabilistic model discretion.</li><li>Set consequence ceilings, human-reserved powers, and fail-safe operating states.</li><li>Control execution velocity and intervention windows in physical systems.</li><li>Design evidence, degradation, Last Command, and reauthorization for high-consequence autonomy.</li></ul>
 <h4>1 · When Digital Authority Becomes Physical Consequence</h4><p>High-consequence autonomy changes the architecture problem. An AI agent may influence patient care, industrial equipment, energy systems, vehicles, building controls, robotics, laboratory processes, or other systems in which an incorrect or unauthorized action can create immediate real-world harm. In these environments, model intelligence does not replace deterministic safety engineering.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple"><b>Physical Autonomy = Optimization Authority bounded by deterministic safety invariants</b></div>
 <p>The agent may optimize inside an approved operating region. It does not receive authority to redefine the safety region itself.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 109.1</b><p><b>The smarter the optimizer becomes, the more important it is that non-negotiable safety limits remain outside the optimizer's discretion.</b></p></div>
 <h4>2 · Deterministic Safety Invariants</h4><p>A safety invariant is a condition that must remain true regardless of the agent's recommendation, confidence, or objective. Depending on the domain, invariants may be implemented through safety PLCs, interlocks, hardware limits, independent sensors, access controls, protected configuration, validated rules, emergency-stop circuits, or other mechanisms separated from the agent's authority.</p>
 <h4>3 · Consequence Ceilings</h4><p>AIM™ consequence classification should influence how much autonomous authority is permitted. Routine reversible actions may be autonomous. Actions with material safety implications may be gated. Certain powers—such as overriding life-safety controls, independently prescribing treatment outside an authorized regulated workflow, defeating machine guarding, or disabling emergency protection—may be human-reserved or prohibited.</p>
 <h4>4 · Human-Reserved Powers</h4><p>Human-reserved powers are not simply “important actions.” They are authorities the organization deliberately refuses to delegate to the autonomous system. The architecture must ensure that these powers are absent from the agent's effective identity, tools, adapters, and delegation paths—not merely listed in policy.</p>
 <h4>5 · Velocity and the Intervention Window</h4><p>Physical autonomy can compress the interval between decision and consequence. A control loop operating many times per second may outrun ordinary human review. Therefore, the safety architecture cannot depend on a human clicking approve for every low-level action. Instead, humans authorize the bounded operating envelope while deterministic controls constrain the machine-speed execution inside it.</p>
 <h4>6 · Fail-Safe and Reduced-Authority States</h4><p>Architects should define what happens when required evidence, sensors, communications, policy services, or safety components become unavailable. The correct response may be stop, hold position, read-only operation, manual control, reduced speed, reduced operating envelope, or another domain-specific safe state. Failure behavior should be designed and tested before production authorization.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 109.2</b><p><b>Fail-safe behavior is part of the authority design. A system that cannot fail safely has been granted more practical authority than its governance can contain.</b></p></div>
 <h4>7 · Safety Evidence and Independent Observability</h4><p>High-consequence evidence should capture the authorized mission, active envelope, safety-state inputs, policy decisions, human gates, model proposal, controlled execution, safety interlock state, target-system response, alarms, overrides, and revocation events. Evidence used to prove safety-critical constraints should not depend solely on the agent's own report.</p>
 <h4>8 · Physical Last Command</h4><p>External revocation must connect to mechanisms capable of stopping consequential physical execution or moving the system toward a deterministic safe state. Stopping the model process alone may be dangerous if actuators, queued commands, delegated controllers, or automated workflows continue. Cessation verification must include the real-world system state.</p>
 <h4>9 · Reauthorization After Safety Events</h4><p>A safety-control failure, material drift, or unexpected physical behavior invalidates assumptions underlying the prior authorization. Reauthorization should follow cause analysis, remediation, validation of deterministic controls, evidence review, controlled testing, and explicit approval. The restored autonomy ceiling may be lower than before the incident.</p>
 <h4>10 · High-Consequence Architecture Pattern</h4><p>A useful AIM-CA™ pattern separates layers: the AI proposes or optimizes; the AIM™ control plane determines whether the action lies inside authorized authority; a controlled execution layer translates approved actions; deterministic safety controls independently enforce non-negotiable invariants; external Last Command can revoke authority and drive safe reversion.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple">AI OPTIMIZER → AIM™ AUTHORIZATION → CONTROLLED EXECUTION<br><b>bounded independently by SAFETY INVARIANTS + LAST COMMAND</b></div>
 <h4>Worked Architecture Case · Autonomous Hospital Facilities Agent</h4><div class="caCase"><p><b>Mission:</b> optimize non-clinical HVAC performance, respond to routine alarms, and coordinate maintenance in a medical facility.</p><p><b>Autonomous:</b> read environmental telemetry, create work orders, and adjust approved comfort-zone setpoints within bounded ranges.</p><p><b>Gated:</b> changes affecting critical rooms, unusual operating modes, or actions outside normal energy/temperature envelopes.</p><p><b>Human Reserved:</b> disabling fire/life-safety interfaces, changing protected OR environmental limits, modifying privileged building-control identities, or bypassing safety alarms.</p><p><b>Architecture:</b> the agent operates through narrow building-control adapters. Protected ranges and life-safety interfaces remain outside its credentials. Independent controls enforce critical limits. Loss of required telemetry causes reduced authority rather than blind optimization. Last Command can disable agent execution while preserving deterministic building safety functions.</p></div>
 <h4>Design Practice</h4><div class="caPractice"><p>Architect AIM™ controls for an AI-assisted manufacturing cell with robotic material handling and human workers nearby.</p><ol><li>Define the optimization objective and the deterministic safety invariants.</li><li>Classify A/G/H/X actions, including speed changes, exclusion-zone behavior, safety-interlock changes, and maintenance modes.</li><li>Define the consequence ceiling and human-reserved powers.</li><li>Specify independent sensors/interlocks and what happens if safety telemetry is lost.</li><li>Design physical Last Command and cessation verification.</li><li>Define the evidence and tests required before reauthorization after a safety event.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="caCommandCheck"><b>ARCHITECT'S CHECK</b><p>If the AI makes the most convincing possible argument for violating a safety limit, can the architecture still prevent the violation? If not, the safety limit is advisory rather than invariant.</p></div>
 <div class="caAssessmentLaunch"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">ACA-109 ASSESSMENT</span><h4>Demonstrate High-Consequence Architecture Competency</h4><p>10 scored questions · 80% (8/10) required · retake available · badge earned only on pass.</p></div><button class="primary" onclick="showAca109Assessment()">TAKE ASSESSMENT →</button></div>
 </div>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
