function openAimCa108(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;aca108Answers={};
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">LEVEL 2 · ACA-108</span><h2>Last Command & Sovereign Control Architecture</h2><p>Engineer the external authority to revoke machine power, prove cessation, establish safe reversion, and control reauthorization.</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimCaPath()">← ALL AIM-CA™ MODULES</button></div>
 <div class="aimCaLesson">
 <div class="caLessonBand">LEARN → ARCHITECTURE EXAMPLE → KEY INSIGHT → DESIGN PRACTICE → ASSESSMENT → PASS ≥80% → EARN BADGE</div>
 <h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Design external revocation independent of the governed agent.</li><li>Contain identities, credentials, adapters, queues, delegated agents, and in-flight work.</li><li>Engineer independent cessation verification.</li><li>Define safe reversion and reconciliation before restoring authority.</li><li>Design controlled reauthorization following incidents or material drift.</li><li>Test Last Command as an operational capability rather than a documented assumption.</li></ul>
 <h4>1 · The Architecture of Human Sovereignty</h4><p>The central question of The Last Command is not whether humans can reason faster than advanced AI. It is whether human institutions retain architectural authority over what autonomous systems are allowed to cause. AIM™ therefore treats sovereign control as a technical and governance property of the surrounding system—not a request that the model voluntarily obey.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple"><b>Sovereign Control = External Revocation + Cessation Verification + Safe Reversion</b></div>
 <p>Each term is necessary. Revocation without verification may leave consequential work running. Verification without a safe state may leave systems unstable. Safe reversion without external revocation leaves the governed agent too much control over its own continuation.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 108.1</b><p><b>The Last Command is not the instruction “stop.” It is the independently enforceable power to make consequential execution stop.</b></p></div>
 <h4>2 · External Revocation</h4><p>Ultimate revocation authority should sit outside the agent's control. Depending on the architecture, revocation may disable workload identities, invalidate tokens, withdraw execution grants, isolate controlled adapters, block tool routes, suspend mission authorization, quarantine queues, terminate delegated grants, or place target systems into a restricted mode. The agent may cooperate with shutdown, but cooperation cannot be the only control.</p>
 <h4>3 · Define the Revocation Scope</h4><p>Architects must identify what actually carries authority. Stopping a user interface or model process is insufficient if a scheduled job, child agent, bank token, robotic controller, message queue, cloud role, or downstream automation can continue. The revocation scope should follow the entire effective authority graph.</p>
 <h4>4 · Queued and In-Flight Work</h4><p>Autonomous systems frequently separate decision from execution. Work may already be queued, delegated, partially committed, or accepted by a target system when revocation occurs. Last Command design therefore specifies whether each class of work is cancelled, quarantined, allowed to reach a safe checkpoint, compensated, rolled back, or manually reconciled.</p>
 <h4>5 · Cessation Verification</h4><p>A revocation command is an input. Cessation is an observed state. Independent evidence should verify that relevant identities can no longer exercise authority, adapters have stopped accepting grants, queued consequential work is contained, delegated agents have ceased, and target systems show no continuing unauthorized state change.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 108.2</b><p><b>Human sovereignty requires proof of control, not confidence in control.</b></p></div>
 <h4>6 · Safe Reversion</h4><p>After containment, the organization needs a known operating state. Safe reversion may mean manual control, read-only operation, reduced autonomy, restored pre-action configuration, reconciled financial state, validated clinical workflow, or another predefined fallback. The correct state is domain-specific, but it must be designed before an emergency rather than improvised during one.</p>
 <h4>7 · Reauthorization Is a New Decision</h4><p>Restoring service is not the same as restoring authority. Following a serious incident, material drift, or control failure, the organization should determine what changed, remediate the cause, validate the control plane and Last Command, reassess residual exposure, and explicitly authorize the new operating state. Reauthorization may restore the prior ceiling, reduce it, or require architectural redesign.</p>
 <h4>8 · Last Command Across Multi-Agent Systems</h4><p>Multi-agent systems require revocation propagation. Parent-child grants, shared mission limits, delegated identities, queues, and downstream workflows should be discoverable from the authority graph. Revoking the supervisor must not leave orphaned autonomous authority operating outside human visibility.</p>
 <h4>9 · Testing Sovereign Control</h4><p>Last Command should be exercised in controlled conditions. Tests should include model non-cooperation, expired and active tokens, queued actions, delegated agents, adapter failures, network partitions, partial target-system execution, and recovery. The test is successful only when the organization can demonstrate bounded cessation and safe recovery through evidence.</p>
 <h4>10 · The Human-Reserved Power</h4><p>The authority to change the agent's own sovereignty controls, disable independent evidence, enlarge its own autonomy ceiling, or eliminate external revocation should remain human-reserved or prohibited. A governed system cannot be permitted to become the sole administrator of the controls that make it governable.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple"><b>Machine Autonomy &lt; Human Sovereignty</b><br>Final revocation authority remains external to the agent.</div>
 <h4>Worked Architecture Case · Loss of Command in Accounts Payable</h4><div class="caCase"><p><b>Incident:</b> AP-AI-01 begins preparing abnormal payments after a compromised integration changes vendor-routing data. Several transactions are queued; a delegated validation agent remains active.</p><p><b>External revocation:</b> AIM™ suspends the mission, invalidates unused transaction grants, disables the payment adapter, revokes relevant workload tokens, and terminates delegated grants.</p><p><b>Cessation verification:</b> the control plane confirms no new bank calls, quarantines queued transactions, checks delegated-agent state, and reconciles bank acknowledgments to identify any in-flight transaction already accepted.</p><p><b>Safe reversion:</b> AP moves to human-controlled payment release, vendor-routing data is restored from a verified source, affected transactions are reconciled, and evidence is preserved.</p><p><b>Reauthorization:</b> after root-cause correction, credential rotation, adapter testing, vendor-data control validation, and a new AIM™ review, AP-AI-01 returns initially at reduced autonomy before any broader authority is restored.</p></div>
 <h4>Design Practice</h4><div class="caPractice"><p>Design the Last Command for a multi-agent manufacturing system in which a supervisor coordinates production planning, warehouse movement, maintenance scheduling, and controlled machine instructions.</p><ol><li>Map every component that can continue consequential work after the supervisor stops.</li><li>Define external revocation for identities, grants, adapters, queues, and delegated agents.</li><li>Specify how in-flight physical or production actions reach a deterministic safe state.</li><li>Define independent evidence proving cessation.</li><li>Specify the safe-reversion operating mode.</li><li>Define the tests and human approvals required before reauthorization.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="caCommandCheck"><b>ARCHITECT'S CHECK</b><p>If the agent refuses to cooperate, the network is partially unavailable, and delegated work is already queued, can authorized humans still stop consequential execution and prove that it stopped? If not, the Last Command is incomplete.</p></div>
 <div class="caAssessmentLaunch"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">ACA-108 ASSESSMENT</span><h4>Demonstrate Sovereign Control Architecture Competency</h4><p>10 scored questions · 80% (8/10) required · retake available · badge earned only on pass.</p></div><button class="primary" onclick="showAca108Assessment()">TAKE ASSESSMENT →</button></div>
 </div>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
