function openAimCa107(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;aca107Answers={};
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">LEVEL 2 · ACA-107</span><h2>Evidence, Observability & Drift Engineering</h2><p>Engineer independent evidence and runtime observability so approved authority can be compared continuously with effective authority.</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimCaPath()">← ALL AIM-CA™ MODULES</button></div>
 <div class="aimCaLesson">
 <div class="caLessonBand">LEARN → ARCHITECTURE EXAMPLE → KEY INSIGHT → DESIGN PRACTICE → ASSESSMENT → PASS ≥80% → EARN BADGE</div>
 <h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Design an evidence chain from proposal through authorization, execution, outcome, and revocation.</li><li>Distinguish behavioral drift, privilege drift, authority drift, and system/context drift.</li><li>Compare approved authority with effective runtime authority continuously.</li><li>Engineer correlated observability across agents, identities, tools, adapters, queues, and target systems.</li><li>Define drift thresholds that reduce autonomy, escalate, or require reauthorization.</li><li>Verify Last Command cessation using independent runtime evidence.</li></ul>
 <h4>1 · Evidence Is a Control Requirement</h4><p>Autonomous systems cannot be governed responsibly if consequential actions cannot be reconstructed. AIM™ evidence is more than conventional application logging. It is an independent record of the authority state under which an action occurred: what the agent proposed, which identity and mission were active, which Authority Envelope and policy version applied, which gate or human approval authorized the action, what controlled adapter executed, and what state actually changed.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple">PROPOSAL <b>→</b> AUTHORIZATION <b>→</b> EXECUTION <b>→</b> OUTCOME <b>→</b> VERIFICATION</div>
 <h4>2 · The AIM™ Black Box</h4><p>The Black Box is the reviewable evidence record for consequential machine operation. It should not attempt to preserve private model chain-of-thought. Instead, it records the governance facts needed for accountability: mission, actor, structured proposal, material evidence, uncertainty where relevant, policy decision, envelope version, approver, execution grant, tool/adapter invocation, target-system response, exceptions, delegated actors, revocation events, and final operating state.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 107.1</b><p><b>If an organization cannot reconstruct the authority that produced an action, it cannot reliably prove that the action was governed.</b></p></div>
 <h4>3 · Observability Must Follow Authority</h4><p>Traditional monitoring often asks whether a service is available, fast, or error-free. AIM-CA™ observability additionally asks whether machine authority remains within its approved design. Telemetry should therefore cover identities, scopes, tools, systems, action classes, transaction amounts, delegation relationships, velocity, aggregate exposure, consequence classes, gates, exceptions, and revocation state.</p>
 <h4>4 · Four Forms of Drift</h4><p><b>Behavioral drift</b> occurs when the agent's operating pattern changes materially. <b>Privilege drift</b> occurs when identities, roles, scopes, credentials, or tool permissions change. <b>Authority drift</b> is the broader divergence between approved authority and what the deployed system can effectively cause. <b>System/context drift</b> occurs when connected systems, workflows, data, regulations, mission assumptions, or consequence conditions change enough that the original authorization is no longer a reliable basis for operation.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple"><b>Production Authority(t) = Approved Passport − Drift(t), subject to expiry and tripwires</b></div>
 <p>This AIM™ expression is a governance model rather than a physical measurement equation. It emphasizes that production authority should not be treated as permanently equal to the original approval when material drift has accumulated.</p>
 <h4>5 · Baseline Versus Effective Authority</h4><p>At authorization time, the organization establishes an approved baseline: Agent Passport, Authority Envelope, identities, roles, tools, systems, autonomy ceiling, delegation depth, consequence constraints, and Last Command controls. Runtime observability continuously compares that baseline with effective state. A new API scope, inherited cloud role, additional tool, changed bank permission, new delegated agent, or widened system interface may represent material drift even if no harmful action has yet occurred.</p>
 <h4>6 · Drift Tripwires</h4><p>Not every change has equal consequence. Architects define tripwires that determine the response. Low-consequence variance may generate evidence and review. Material privilege or system changes may automatically reduce autonomy. High-consequence drift may suspend execution and require human reauthorization. The response should be designed before the drift event rather than improvised after it.</p>
 <h4>7 · Correlation Across Multi-Agent Systems</h4><p>Every mission and delegated action should carry stable correlation identifiers so the organization can reconstruct the authority graph. Parent-child delegation, authorization decisions, tool calls, execution grants, queue events, target-system state changes, and revocations should be traceable as one governed chain rather than isolated log files.</p>
 <h4>8 · Evidence Quality</h4><p>Evidence should be timestamped, attributable, sufficiently tamper-resistant for the consequence class, retained according to policy, and separated from the agent's ability to rewrite its own history. The system should distinguish a proposal from an authorization and an authorization from proof of execution. A model claiming “payment completed” is not equivalent to the bank or controlled adapter confirming the transaction state.</p>
 <h4>9 · Drift and Reauthorization</h4><p>Authorization is based on assumptions. When material drift changes those assumptions, the correct response may be reassessment through AIM™ Dx, architectural correction through AIM™ Rx, reduced autonomy, or formal reauthorization. A production approval should not become a permanent license for a system whose effective authority has materially changed.</p>
 <h4>10 · Cessation Verification</h4><p>Last Command evidence must answer a critical question: <b>Did consequential execution actually stop?</b> A revocation event is only the beginning. Observability should verify adapter shutdown, credential invalidation, queue containment, delegated-agent cessation, termination of in-flight privileged work where possible, and establishment of a safe state.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 107.2</b><p><b>“Stop requested” is an event. “Execution ceased” is evidence.</b></p></div>
 <h4>Worked Architecture Case · Enterprise IT Response Agent</h4><div class="caCase"><p><b>Approved baseline:</b> IT-AI may restart five named services, collect approved diagnostics, and open incidents. It cannot change firewall policy, rotate privileged credentials, disable security controls, or create new production identities.</p><p><b>Observed drift:</b> A platform update causes the service identity to inherit a new role that permits production firewall modification.</p><p><b>AIM-CA response:</b> Runtime reconciliation detects the new effective permission against the approved Agent Passport. A privilege-drift tripwire marks the variance as material because it creates a new consequential action path. The control plane prevents use of the new permission, reduces or suspends affected autonomy, records the drift event, and routes the system for review. The permission is removed or separately authorized before normal autonomy is restored.</p><p><b>Black Box:</b> records baseline version, detected role change, effective permission, tripwire, policy response, human review, remediation, verification, and reauthorization state.</p></div>
 <h4>Design Practice</h4><div class="caPractice"><p>Design evidence and drift controls for AP-AI-01, an accounts-payable agent operating in a high-volume enterprise environment.</p><ol><li>Define the Black Box fields from invoice proposal through bank result.</li><li>Establish the approved identity, tool, financial, system, and delegation baseline.</li><li>Create tripwires for new vendor-bank permissions, increased transaction velocity, new payment adapters, and delegation changes.</li><li>Define which drift conditions alert, reduce autonomy, suspend operation, or require reauthorization.</li><li>Correlate parent/child and queued payment activity.</li><li>Define evidence proving that financial execution ceased after Last Command activation.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="caCommandCheck"><b>ARCHITECT'S CHECK</b><p>Can you prove—not merely assume—that the authority running in production today is the authority that was approved? If not, observability is incomplete.</p></div>
 <div class="caAssessmentLaunch"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">ACA-107 ASSESSMENT</span><h4>Demonstrate Evidence & Drift Engineering Competency</h4><p>10 scored questions · 80% (8/10) required · retake available · badge earned only on pass.</p></div><button class="primary" onclick="showAca107Assessment()">TAKE ASSESSMENT →</button></div>
 </div>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
