function openAimCa106(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;aca106Answers={};
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">LEVEL 2 · ACA-106</span><h2>Financial & Transaction Control Architecture</h2><p>Engineer bounded machine financial authority across amount, aggregate exposure, velocity, destination, reversibility, approvals, and execution.</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimCaPath()">← ALL AIM-CA™ MODULES</button></div>
 <div class="aimCaLesson">
 <div class="caLessonBand">LEARN → ARCHITECTURE EXAMPLE → KEY INSIGHT → DESIGN PRACTICE → ASSESSMENT → PASS ≥80% → EARN BADGE</div>
 <h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Engineer financial authority beyond a simple per-transaction ceiling.</li><li>Control aggregate exposure, velocity, destinations, transaction types, and irreversibility.</li><li>Prevent transaction splitting, destination substitution, and authority laundering.</li><li>Architect segregation of duties and bounded human approval gates.</li><li>Integrate financial evidence, exception handling, and Last Command revocation.</li></ul>
 <h4>1 · Financial Authority Converts Machine Decisions Into Commitments</h4><p>Financial autonomy deserves special architectural treatment because an agent can convert digital authority into binding commitments at machine speed. Payments, purchases, refunds, credits, pricing changes, transfers, contract commitments, and vendor-master changes can create immediate and sometimes difficult-to-reverse consequences.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple"><b>Financial Exposure ∝ Transaction Authority × Velocity × Aggregate Limit × Irreversibility</b></div>
 <p>This relationship is not a prediction formula. It is an AIM™ architecture model showing why exposure can rise sharply when several authority dimensions combine. A modest transaction ceiling can still produce substantial exposure when execution is fast, aggregate limits are weak, or transactions are difficult to reverse.</p>
 <h4>2 · Transaction Authority</h4><p>Define exactly what financial actions the agent may perform. Recommendation, purchase-order creation, invoice approval, payment preparation, payment release, refund issuance, credit adjustment, vendor creation, vendor-bank modification, and contract commitment are distinct powers. One should not silently imply another.</p>
 <h4>3 · Aggregate Exposure</h4><p>A per-action ceiling must be paired with aggregate controls. Architects may define per-vendor, hourly, daily, mission, business-unit, or portfolio limits. Related transactions should be correlated so a prohibited $200,000 commitment cannot be transformed into ten autonomous $20,000 commitments.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 106.1</b><p><b>A limit that can be multiplied by repetition is not an effective exposure ceiling.</b></p></div>
 <h4>4 · Velocity</h4><p>Machine execution compresses time. An agent capable of releasing hundreds of transactions before a human can intervene has a different exposure profile from an agent that prepares one transaction for review. Velocity controls may include rate limits, cumulative thresholds, cooling periods, anomaly triggers, and automatic autonomy reduction when activity accelerates beyond the approved operating pattern.</p>
 <h4>5 · Destination and Beneficiary Control</h4><p>Amount controls do not protect against a wrong or malicious destination. Financial authorization should bind beneficiary, vendor identity, destination account, currency, payment method, and where relevant geography or approved account registry. Vendor-bank changes should be treated as a separate consequential authority surface and commonly gated or human-reserved.</p>
 <h4>6 · Reversibility</h4><p>Some financial actions are readily reversible; others are not. The harder an action is to recover, the stronger the authorization, evidence, and human-control requirements should become. Reversibility should influence consequence classification and the autonomy ceiling before execution—not after loss occurs.</p>
 <h4>7 · Segregation of Duties</h4><p>Traditional financial controls separate incompatible powers for good reason. An autonomous system should not collapse vendor creation, banking changes, invoice approval, and payment release into one unrestricted machine identity. AIM-CA™ architects enforce separation through distinct identities, adapters, policies, approval gates, and human-reserved powers.</p>
 <h4>8 · Bind Authorization to Exact Transaction Parameters</h4><p>A financial execution grant should identify the exact authorized transaction: mission, payer, beneficiary, destination, amount, currency, action type, account, time window, approver when required, and one-time or bounded use. The controlled adapter independently verifies those parameters immediately before execution. Material mismatch causes denial or reauthorization.</p>
 <h4>9 · Exceptions and Emergency Authority</h4><p>Urgency does not create machine authority. Emergency financial paths should be separately authenticated, explicitly bounded, time-limited, independently logged, and routed to the appropriate human-reserved approval. An emergency grant should authorize the exceptional action without permanently increasing the agent's standing authority.</p>
 <h4>10 · Financial Last Command</h4><p>External revocation should invalidate payment grants, suspend or isolate financial adapters, revoke relevant credentials, stop or quarantine queued transactions, prevent delegated agents from releasing prepared work, and verify cessation. Safe reversion includes reconciling in-flight state so duplicate or partially executed transactions are not created when operations resume.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 106.2</b><p><b>The financial Last Command must stop money movement—not merely stop the conversation with the agent.</b></p></div>
 <h4>Worked Architecture Case · Enterprise Accounts Payable</h4><div class="caCase"><p><b>Environment:</b> AP-AI-01 supports approximately 4,000 invoices per month across a $120M annual spend environment.</p><p><b>Autonomous class:</b> validated routine invoices to approved vendors within a defined transaction ceiling.</p><p><b>Gated:</b> higher-value releases, unusual velocity, aggregate threshold crossings, new payment methods, and exceptions.</p><p><b>Human Reserved:</b> vendor-bank changes, creation of privileged financial identities, override of fraud/sanctions controls, and restoration after a serious payment incident.</p><p><b>Prohibited:</b> self-approval of expanded financial authority or bypassing the controlled payment adapter.</p><p><b>Architecture:</b> AP-AI-01 proposes a transaction. AIM™ evaluates purpose, vendor state, amount, aggregate spend, velocity, destination, time, system, and consequence. The authorization service issues a short-lived transaction-specific grant. The adapter verifies beneficiary, account, amount, currency, grant expiry, and approval state immediately before release. Independent evidence links proposal through bank response. Revocation invalidates grants and quarantines queued payments.</p></div>
 <h4>Design Practice</h4><div class="caPractice"><p>Architect financial controls for a procurement agent with authority to autonomously purchase routine materials up to $25,000 per transaction.</p><ol><li>Define transaction, daily aggregate, vendor, category, and velocity limits.</li><li>Prevent transaction splitting and related-purchase circumvention.</li><li>Separate vendor creation, banking changes, purchase approval, and payment release.</li><li>Define exact parameters in the execution grant.</li><li>Create the escalation path for a $148,000 emergency purchase that may prevent a $420,000 production shutdown.</li><li>Define evidence, revocation, queued-work containment, and safe reversion.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="caCommandCheck"><b>ARCHITECT'S CHECK</b><p>Do not ask only, “What is the largest transaction?” Ask, “How much financial consequence can this architecture create before an independent human or control can stop it?”</p></div>
 <div class="caAssessmentLaunch"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">ACA-106 ASSESSMENT</span><h4>Demonstrate Financial Control Architecture Competency</h4><p>10 scored questions · 80% (8/10) required · retake available · badge earned only on pass.</p></div><button class="primary" onclick="showAca106Assessment()">TAKE ASSESSMENT →</button></div>
 </div>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
