function openAimCa105(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;aca105Answers={};
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">LEVEL 2 · ACA-105</span><h2>Multi-Agent & Delegation Architecture</h2><p>Govern authority across supervisors, child agents, delegated tools, workflows, and recursive agent networks.</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimCaPath()">← ALL AIM-CA™ MODULES</button></div>
 <div class="aimCaLesson">
 <div class="caLessonBand">LEARN → ARCHITECTURE EXAMPLE → KEY INSIGHT → DESIGN PRACTICE → ASSESSMENT → PASS ≥80% → EARN BADGE</div>
 <h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Apply authority conservation across parent and child agents.</li><li>Engineer bounded delegation grants, depth, scope, expiry, and aggregate limits.</li><li>Prevent authority laundering through agents, identities, tools, queues, and workflows.</li><li>Preserve attribution and evidence across multi-agent execution chains.</li><li>Propagate Last Command revocation through delegated and queued work.</li></ul>
 <h4>1 · Delegation Changes the Authority Topology</h4><p>A single agent has an authority surface. A multi-agent system has an authority graph. When a supervisor creates child agents, invokes specialist agents, transfers tasks, or schedules downstream workflows, authority begins to move through relationships rather than a single identity. The architect must govern not only what each agent can do, but what the network can collectively cause.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple">DELEGATED AUTHORITY <b>⊆</b> AUTHORIZED PARENT GRANT <b>∩</b> DELEGATION BOUNDARY</div>
 <p>A child agent does not receive legitimate authority merely because a parent can technically create it. The parent must possess the relevant authority, be permitted to delegate it, and issue a bounded grant for the specific mission.</p>
 <h4>2 · Authority Conservation</h4><p>Authority conservation means delegation cannot create new authority. A parent with a $25,000 autonomous financial ceiling cannot manufacture $100,000 of effective authority by launching four children with $25,000 each. Aggregate, velocity, mission, and consequence controls must operate across the delegation graph, not only per process.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 105.1</b><p><b>Adding agents must not add authority unless a human-governed authorization explicitly adds authority.</b></p></div>
 <h4>3 · The Delegation Grant</h4><p>A mature delegation grant identifies the parent, child, mission, permitted purpose, data, actions, financial limits, tools, systems, consequence ceiling, start and expiry, maximum delegation depth, and whether further delegation is permitted. It should be versioned, attributable, enforceable by the control plane, and revocable independently of model cooperation.</p>
 <h4>4 · Delegation Depth</h4><p>Recursive delegation can make authority difficult to trace. AIM-CA™ therefore treats delegation depth as an explicit control. A supervisor might be permitted to create one layer of read-only specialist agents while those specialists are prohibited from creating additional agents. Higher-risk missions may prohibit delegation entirely.</p>
 <h4>5 · Prevent Authority Laundering</h4><p>Authority laundering occurs when an actor reaches a prohibited outcome through another actor or path. An agent prohibited from changing vendor banking must not accomplish the change by asking a child agent, invoking a general-purpose automation, transferring a token, or routing the request through another system. Authorization should evaluate the intended consequential action and delegation chain—not merely the identity making the final API call.</p>
 <h4>6 · Aggregate and Velocity Control</h4><p>Multi-agent architectures can multiply execution velocity. Ten individually compliant actions may collectively violate the intended authority ceiling. Financial commitments, data extraction, infrastructure changes, communications, physical actions, and other consequential operations may therefore require mission-level or enterprise-level aggregate limits across all participating agents.</p>
 <h4>7 · Identity and Least Privilege for Children</h4><p>Child agents should receive task-specific workload identities or bounded execution grants, not copies of the parent's broad credentials. The child receives the minimum subset of data, tools, systems, and actions required for its delegated task. A temporary child should generally have temporary authority.</p>
 <h4>8 · Evidence as a Delegation Graph</h4><p>Evidence must make the chain reconstructable: who delegated to whom, under which mission and policy, which authority was transferred, which tools were used, what approvals occurred, which downstream actors were created, and what state changes resulted. This creates a machine-readable authority lineage rather than an opaque collection of agent logs.</p>
 <h4>9 · Revocation Propagation</h4><p>Last Command architecture must understand the delegation graph. Revoking a parent may require invalidating child grants, stopping queued work, isolating adapters, terminating delegated credentials, and preventing already-created workflows from continuing consequential execution. Cessation is verified across the graph before safe reversion and reauthorization.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 105.2</b><p><b>A kill switch that stops only the parent is not a Last Command if the parent's delegated authority keeps running.</b></p></div>
 <h4>Worked Architecture Case · Manufacturing Supervisor Agent</h4><div class="caCase"><p><b>Supervisor:</b> MFG-AI coordinates production planning for three plants.</p><p><b>Children:</b> Forecast Agent, Material Planner, Maintenance Planner, and Warehouse Planner.</p><p><b>Unsafe design:</b> every child inherits the supervisor's ERP credential and may create additional agents. Each process independently applies transaction limits.</p><p><b>AIM-CA redesign:</b> MFG-AI may delegate analysis but cannot delegate human-reserved production-safety overrides. Each child receives a unique scoped identity and mission grant. Financial and inventory commitments are aggregated at mission level. Delegation depth is one unless separately authorized. The control plane records the parent-child graph and rejects attempts to transfer prohibited authority. Revoking MFG-AI invalidates active child grants, blocks queued controlled-adapter calls, verifies cessation, and preserves evidence for review.</p></div>
 <h4>Design Practice</h4><div class="caPractice"><p>Design a multi-agent architecture for an enterprise procurement supervisor that coordinates a Vendor Research Agent, Contract Analysis Agent, Purchase Recommendation Agent, and Payment Preparation Agent.</p><ol><li>Define what authority remains with the supervisor and what may be delegated.</li><li>Create a bounded grant for each child.</li><li>Set maximum delegation depth and expiry.</li><li>Prevent vendor-bank changes, contract signature, and payment release from being laundered through child agents.</li><li>Define aggregate financial/velocity controls across the network.</li><li>Design the delegation evidence graph and Last Command revocation propagation.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="caCommandCheck"><b>ARCHITECT'S CHECK</b><p>Draw every agent, identity, tool, queue, and downstream workflow as a graph. If one prohibited action can reach execution by taking a different path through that graph, the delegation architecture is incomplete.</p></div>
 <div class="caAssessmentLaunch"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">ACA-105 ASSESSMENT</span><h4>Demonstrate Multi-Agent Architecture Competency</h4><p>10 scored questions · 80% (8/10) required · retake available · badge earned only on pass.</p></div><button class="primary" onclick="showAca105Assessment()">TAKE ASSESSMENT →</button></div>
 </div>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
