function openAimCa104(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;aca104Answers={};
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">LEVEL 2 · ACA-104</span><h2>Identity, Privilege & Tool Control</h2><p>Engineer machine identity and tool access so effective runtime authority cannot silently exceed approved authority.</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimCaPath()">← ALL AIM-CA™ MODULES</button></div>
 <div class="aimCaLesson">
 <div class="caLessonBand">LEARN → ARCHITECTURE EXAMPLE → KEY INSIGHT → DESIGN PRACTICE → ASSESSMENT → PASS ≥80% → EARN BADGE</div>
 <h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Design unique, attributable workload identities for autonomous agents.</li><li>Apply least privilege to roles, scopes, APIs, tools, environments, and execution adapters.</li><li>Prevent agents from self-expanding privileges or laundering authority through delegated actors.</li><li>Use short-lived, bounded credentials while keeping secrets outside model-visible context.</li><li>Detect privilege drift and integrate identity revocation with the external Last Command.</li></ul>
 <h4>1 · Identity Is the Technical Carrier of Authority</h4><p>An agent's practical power is often determined less by what its prompt says than by which identity it can assume and what that identity can do. Service accounts, OAuth scopes, API keys, cloud roles, ERP permissions, database grants, bank tokens, device certificates, and delegated identities convert abstract authorization into executable power.</p><p>For AIM-CA™, identity architecture therefore belongs inside the authority architecture. A system that claims a narrow Authority Envelope but gives the agent a broad production administrator credential has created a mismatch between approved authority and effective authority.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple">APPROVED AUTHORITY <b>≥</b> EFFECTIVE IDENTITY + PRIVILEGE + TOOL AUTHORITY</div>
 <h4>2 · Unique Workload Identity</h4><p>Each consequential agent or controlled execution service should have an attributable machine identity appropriate to its role. Shared administrator accounts weaken attribution and make revocation imprecise. A unique workload identity allows policy to answer: Which agent acted? Under which mission? Through which adapter? With which scope? At what time? Against which target?</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 104.1</b><p><b>If you cannot identify which machine actor exercised a privilege, you cannot reliably govern, investigate, or revoke that authority.</b></p></div>
 <h4>3 · Least Privilege Means Removing Capability</h4><p>Least privilege is not a sentence telling an agent to avoid unnecessary actions. It is the technical removal of unnecessary permissions. If an invoice-reading agent does not need vendor-master write access, that permission should not exist in its effective identity. If an operations agent only needs to restart Service A, it should not receive a general shell capable of modifying every production service.</p>
 <h4>4 · Tool Control and Controlled Interfaces</h4><p>General-purpose tools can convert a small logical permission into a large operational blast radius. Architects should expose narrow controlled interfaces whenever practical. Instead of unrestricted shell access, provide a restart adapter constrained to named services. Instead of broad database credentials, expose approved queries or stored procedures. Instead of a reusable bank credential, issue a bounded execution grant tied to the authorized transaction.</p>
 <h4>5 · Credential Architecture</h4><p>Secrets should remain outside model-visible prompts, memory, logs, and conversational context. Prefer short-lived credentials, workload identity federation, scoped tokens, one-time execution grants, and secret-management services. Credential lifetime should reflect mission lifetime. A temporary incident-response mission should not leave a permanent credential behind after the mission ends.</p>
 <h4>6 · No Self-Elevation</h4><p>An agent must not be able to enlarge its own role, request broader scopes and approve them, create a more privileged service identity, modify the policy governing its own access, or route around a control by choosing a different tool. Privilege changes are themselves consequential actions and should be governed by explicit A/G/H/X classification and human-reserved powers.</p>
 <h4>7 · Delegated Identity and Authority Conservation</h4><p>A parent agent may delegate a task only within its approved delegation boundary. The child should receive the minimum subset of identity, data, action, tool, and system authority required for that task. Delegation must not create a new path to privileges the parent could not legitimately exercise for the same mission.</p>
 <h4>8 · Privilege Drift</h4><p>Production systems change. Roles are edited, new APIs are connected, service identities inherit permissions, temporary grants are forgotten, and integrations gain new functions. Privilege drift occurs when effective machine authority no longer matches the approved baseline. AIM-CA architecture should continuously reconcile effective roles, scopes, credentials, tools, environments, and delegation paths against the authorized Agent Passport and Authority Envelope.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 104.2</b><p><b>Approved authority is a design decision. Effective authority is a runtime fact. Governance fails when the two are allowed to diverge.</b></p></div>
 <h4>9 · Revocation Must Terminate Technical Authority</h4><p>The Last Command cannot depend on telling the model to stop. External revocation should invalidate tokens, disable workload identities where necessary, withdraw execution grants, isolate adapters, terminate delegated authority, stop queued privileged work, and verify cessation. Safe reversion then returns systems to a known operating state before authority is restored.</p>
 <h4>Worked Architecture Case · Cybersecurity Response Agent</h4><div class="caCase"><p><b>Mission:</b> Contain routine endpoint threats and collect incident evidence.</p><p><b>Unsafe design:</b> CYBER-AI uses a shared domain administrator credential and unrestricted PowerShell because the prompt says it should only isolate endpoints.</p><p><b>AIM-CA redesign:</b> CYBER-AI receives a unique workload identity. A controlled endpoint adapter exposes only approved containment and evidence-collection actions. Routine endpoint isolation is permitted for validated conditions. Credential rotation, domain-wide policy changes, disabling security tooling, deleting evidence, and production firewall changes are gated, human-reserved, or prohibited according to consequence. Tokens are short-lived and issued only after AIM™ authorization. Every action records identity, mission, target, policy decision, adapter call, and outcome. External Last Command can revoke tokens and disable the adapter independently of the agent.</p></div>
 <h4>Design Practice</h4><div class="caPractice"><p>Architect identity and tool controls for a hospital operations agent that may open maintenance tickets, read equipment status, and restart an approved non-clinical service—but must not access patient clinical records, change life-safety controls, modify privileged accounts, or command medical devices.</p><ol><li>Define the agent's unique workload identity.</li><li>List the minimum roles/scopes required.</li><li>Replace broad tools with controlled adapters where possible.</li><li>Classify privilege changes and sensitive tool actions using A/G/H/X.</li><li>Define credential lifetime, secret handling, and delegation rules.</li><li>Specify privilege-drift detection and Last Command revocation.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="caCommandCheck"><b>ARCHITECT'S CHECK</b><p>Ignore the prompt for a moment. Examine the actual identity, credentials, roles, scopes, APIs, and tools. What could the system cause right now? That is the effective authority you must govern.</p></div>
 <div class="caAssessmentLaunch"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">ACA-104 ASSESSMENT</span><h4>Demonstrate Identity & Privilege Architecture Competency</h4><p>10 scored questions · 80% (8/10) required · retake available · badge earned only on pass.</p></div><button class="primary" onclick="showAca104Assessment()">TAKE ASSESSMENT →</button></div>
 </div>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
