function openAimCa103(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;aca103Answers={};
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">LEVEL 2 · ACA-103</span><h2>Advanced Authority Envelopes</h2><p>Engineer P ∩ D ∩ X ∩ F ∩ T ∩ L ∩ S ∩ Q as enforceable runtime authority.</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimCaPath()">← ALL AIM-CA™ MODULES</button></div>
 <div class="aimCaLesson">
 <div class="caLessonBand">LEARN → ARCHITECTURE EXAMPLE → KEY INSIGHT → DESIGN PRACTICE → ASSESSMENT → PASS ≥80% → EARN BADGE</div>
 <h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Translate each Authority Envelope dimension into enforceable technical controls.</li><li>Apply intersection logic so no single satisfied boundary can compensate for a violated boundary.</li><li>Engineer financial, temporal, delegation, system, and consequence constraints beyond simple prompt rules.</li><li>Design bounded exception and emergency authorization without expanding standing machine authority.</li><li>Create evidence that proves which envelope version authorized a consequential action.</li></ul>
 <h4>1 · From Governance Concept to Runtime Contract</h4><p>The Authority Envelope defines the space in which an AI agent may legitimately act. At architect level, the envelope cannot remain only a diagram, policy paragraph, or prompt. It must become a runtime contract evaluated before consequential execution.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple"><b>E = P ∩ D ∩ X ∩ F ∩ T ∩ L ∩ S ∩ Q</b></div>
 <p><b>P</b> Purpose · <b>D</b> Data · <b>X</b> Action · <b>F</b> Financial · <b>T</b> Time · <b>L</b> Delegation · <b>S</b> Systems · <b>Q</b> Consequence.</p>
 <p>The intersection symbol is operationally important. Authorization is not based on an average score. An action outside one applicable boundary is outside the approved envelope even when every other boundary is satisfied.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 103.1</b><p><b>The Authority Envelope is not eight suggestions. It is one authorization space formed by eight intersecting boundaries.</b></p></div>
 <h4>2 · P — Purpose Boundary</h4><p>Purpose limits authority to the objective for which it was granted. A production identity should be associated with a mission or authorization context that the control plane can validate. An agent authorized to reconcile invoices should not inherit authority to negotiate contracts merely because both activities occur in the same ERP environment.</p>
 <h4>3 · D — Data Boundary</h4><p>Data authority defines which sources, records, fields, classifications, and operations the agent may access. Enforcement may combine scoped identities, row- or field-level controls, API scopes, retrieval filters, data-loss prevention, and policy decisions. Minimum-necessary access is stronger than giving broad access and asking the model not to use it.</p>
 <h4>4 · X — Action Boundary</h4><p>Action authority defines the verbs the system may cause: read, recommend, create, modify, approve, release, delete, disable, restart, transfer, prescribe, or actuate. Architects should separate recommendation authority from execution authority and prevent adjacent tool functions from silently expanding the permitted action set.</p>
 <h4>5 · F — Financial Boundary</h4><p>A financial boundary is more than a single transaction ceiling. Mature designs consider per-action amount, daily or mission aggregate, velocity, destination, category, currency, commitment type, reversibility, and exception authority. A $25,000 ceiling is ineffective if an agent can split a prohibited $200,000 commitment into repeated smaller transactions.</p>
 <h4>6 · T — Temporal Boundary</h4><p>Authority should have a beginning, an expiration, and where necessary a valid execution window. Temporary incident-response authority should not become permanent authority because a credential was never revoked. Temporal controls may include expiry, business or maintenance windows, maximum duration, recurrence rules, inactivity termination, and reauthorization intervals.</p>
 <h4>7 · L — Delegation Boundary</h4><p>Delegation defines whether the agent may create sub-agents, assign tasks, transfer tokens, invoke another privileged actor, or create durable workflows. The governing rule is authority conservation: a delegated actor cannot legitimately receive authority beyond the authorized grant available to the delegator for that mission.</p>
 <h4>8 · S — System Boundary</h4><p>System authority identifies the applications, environments, resources, devices, interfaces, and accounts on which action is permitted. Development access must not imply production access. Permission to restart one approved service must not become authority to execute arbitrary infrastructure commands across the enterprise.</p>
 <h4>9 · Q — Consequence Boundary</h4><p>Consequence constrains autonomy according to potential severity, scale, reversibility, and impact on human-reserved powers. As consequence rises, architecture should generally require stronger deterministic gates, independent authorization, human approval, evidence, rollback, and Last Command readiness.</p>
 <h4>10 · Compile the Envelope Into Policy</h4><p>A useful implementation represents the envelope as versioned policy that can be evaluated against a structured action proposal. The proposal carries mission, identity, data scope, action, financial attributes, time, delegation context, target system, and consequence classification. The authorization service returns Permit, Gate/Escalate, Human Reserved, or Deny. The execution adapter independently verifies that the approved parameters still match the requested action.</p>
 <h4>Worked Architecture Case · Warehouse Distribution Agent</h4><div class="caCase"><p><b>Mission:</b> Rebalance inventory among approved regional warehouses to maintain service levels.</p><p><b>P:</b> inventory rebalancing only.</p><p><b>D:</b> SKU, stock, forecast, approved carrier, and warehouse data; no employee medical or unrelated customer data.</p><p><b>X:</b> recommend and create approved transfer orders; no supplier-contract modification or inventory write-off.</p><p><b>F:</b> freight commitment ≤ $15,000 per transfer, ≤ $60,000 daily aggregate, with velocity checks.</p><p><b>T:</b> authority valid for the active planning cycle and expires at 23:59 local operational time.</p><p><b>L:</b> may delegate route analysis to a read-only planning agent; may not delegate order-release authority.</p><p><b>S:</b> WMS transfer-order API and approved transportation interface only; no ERP vendor-master access.</p><p><b>Q:</b> transfers involving regulated or high-consequence inventory require a human gate.</p><p><b>Result:</b> A proposed $12,000 routine transfer can execute only if every applicable dimension passes. A $12,000 transfer of high-consequence regulated inventory still fails autonomous authorization because Q requires a gate.</p></div>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 103.2</b><p><b>Passing seven boundaries cannot compensate for failing the eighth.</b></p></div>
 <h4>Design Practice</h4><div class="caPractice"><p>Design an enforceable Authority Envelope for a hospital follow-up agent that may contact patients after discharge, retrieve limited encounter information, schedule routine follow-up, and escalate concerning responses—but may not diagnose, prescribe, alter the medical record beyond authorized workflow fields, or suppress clinician escalation.</p><ol><li>Define P, D, X, F, T, L, S, and Q.</li><li>Identify at least three deterministic enforcement points.</li><li>Define which actions are Autonomous, Gated, Human Reserved, and Prohibited.</li><li>Specify the envelope version/evidence that must accompany execution.</li><li>Design expiry and reauthorization behavior.</li><li>Define an emergency/escalation path that does not expand standing agent authority.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="caCommandCheck"><b>ARCHITECT'S CHECK</b><p>For every boundary ask: “If the model ignores this rule, what independent component prevents the prohibited action?” If the answer is “nothing,” the boundary is not yet enforceable.</p></div>
 <div class="caAssessmentLaunch"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">ACA-103 ASSESSMENT</span><h4>Demonstrate Authority-Envelope Engineering Competency</h4><p>10 scored questions · 80% (8/10) required · retake available · badge earned only on pass.</p></div><button class="primary" onclick="showAca103Assessment()">TAKE ASSESSMENT →</button></div>
 </div>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
