function openAimCa102(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;aca102Answers={};
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">LEVEL 2 · ACA-102</span><h2>AIM™ Control-Plane Engineering</h2><p>Engineer the independent authorization layer between intelligent proposal and consequential execution.</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimCaPath()">← ALL AIM-CA™ MODULES</button></div>
 <div class="aimCaLesson">
 <div class="caLessonBand">LEARN → ARCHITECTURE EXAMPLE → KEY INSIGHT → DESIGN PRACTICE → ASSESSMENT → PASS ≥80% → EARN BADGE</div>
 <h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Explain why intelligent proposal and execution authorization must be separated.</li><li>Design the core services of an AIM™ control plane.</li><li>Engineer deterministic policy decisions and controlled execution adapters.</li><li>Bind authorization to exact action parameters, identities, systems, time, and consequence.</li><li>Design evidence, exception, fail-closed, and Last Command behavior into the execution path.</li></ul>
 <h4>1 · The Control Plane Is the Authority Boundary</h4><p>An autonomous agent may discover, reason, plan, and propose. None of those capabilities should automatically create permission to cause a consequential external action. The AIM™ control plane is the independent architectural layer that evaluates a proposed action against the organization's authorized objective, Authority Envelope, A/G/H/X classification, human-reserved powers, consequence class, and current operating state before execution is permitted.</p><p>This separation allows the organization to use increasingly capable models without making model intelligence itself the source of authority.</p>
 <div class="caPrinciple">AGENT PROPOSES <b>→</b> AIM™ AUTHORIZES <b>→</b> CONTROLLED ADAPTER EXECUTES <b>→</b> EVIDENCE VERIFIES</div>
 <h4>2 · Five Core Control-Plane Responsibilities</h4><ol><li><b>Receive a structured action proposal.</b> The proposal identifies actor, mission, action, target, amount or scope, system, time, delegation context, and expected consequence.</li><li><b>Evaluate authorization.</b> Policy checks the proposal against the active Authority Envelope and applicable gates.</li><li><b>Resolve the decision.</b> The result is Autonomous/Permit, Gated/Escalate, Human Reserved, or Prohibited/Deny.</li><li><b>Issue bounded execution authority.</b> A controlled adapter receives only the parameters necessary for the authorized action.</li><li><b>Record and verify evidence.</b> The system records proposal, policy version, authorization, approver where applicable, execution result, exceptions, and cessation state.</li></ol>
 <h4>3 · Policy Decision Point vs. Execution Point</h4><p>The policy decision point answers <b>“May this exact action occur under the current authority?”</b> The execution point answers <b>“Can I safely perform exactly the action that was authorized?”</b> Combining these responsibilities inside the model weakens independence. Separating them creates an architectural checkpoint that can remain stable even when the reasoning model changes.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 102.1</b><p><b>The model may recommend an exception. It must not become the authority that grants its own exception.</b></p></div>
 <h4>4 · Bind Authorization to the Action</h4><p>Authorization should not be a vague reusable approval such as “procurement approved.” It should be bound to the consequential parameters: vendor, amount, account, action type, system, time window, requesting identity, mission, and any required human approval. If execution parameters differ materially from the authorization record, the adapter rejects the action and returns it for a new decision.</p>
 <h4>5 · Controlled Adapters</h4><p>A controlled adapter is deliberately narrower than a general-purpose production credential. It translates an approved action into the target system while independently enforcing constraints. For a payment, the adapter might enforce vendor identity, destination account, maximum amount, currency, transaction type, approval token, expiration time, and one-time use. For infrastructure, it might restrict resource, command family, environment, blast radius, maintenance window, and rollback requirement.</p>
 <h4>6 · Fail-Closed and Exception Engineering</h4><p>When authorization cannot be established for a high-consequence action, uncertainty must not silently become permission. The control plane should deny, pause, or route to the explicitly defined human gate. Emergency paths may exist, but emergency authority must itself be bounded, authenticated, logged, time-limited, and unable to permanently expand the agent's standing authority.</p>
 <h4>7 · Evidence Is Part of the Architecture</h4><p>A mature control plane creates an evidence chain linking proposal → policy decision → authorization → execution → outcome. Evidence should identify the agent and human owner, policy and Authority Envelope version, inputs material to the decision, approver when required, adapter invocation, target-system result, exception state, and revocation events. Logging after the fact is useful; evidence integrated with authorization is stronger because it proves which authority was actually exercised.</p>
 <h4>8 · Last Command Integration</h4><p>The control plane must be subordinate to an external revocation mechanism. Revocation should invalidate execution grants, disable or isolate adapters, stop or quarantine queued work, constrain delegated agents, verify that consequential execution ceased, and establish a safe state. The governed agent should never be the sole mechanism capable of stopping itself.</p>
 <h4>Worked Architecture Case · Autonomous Accounts Payable</h4><div class="caCase"><p><b>Agent:</b> AP-AI-01 processes approximately 4,000 invoices per month.</p><p><b>Authority:</b> routine validated invoices may be processed autonomously up to the approved ceiling; vendor banking changes and high-value releases remain gated or human-reserved.</p><p><b>Unsafe architecture:</b> the agent holds a broad ERP service identity and bank API credential. Policy limits exist primarily in prompts.</p><p><b>AIM-CA redesign:</b> AP-AI-01 produces a structured payment proposal. The AIM™ policy decision point evaluates purpose, vendor, amount, banking-change state, financial ceiling, time, system, delegation, and consequence. An approved transaction produces a short-lived bounded execution grant. The payment adapter verifies the grant against the exact vendor, account, amount, currency, and expiration before calling the bank interface. Any mismatch fails closed. Every stage writes evidence to an independent record. External revocation can invalidate grants and halt the adapter without cooperation from AP-AI-01.</p></div>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 102.2</b><p><b>A permission should authorize an action—not hand the agent a reusable pathway around the control plane.</b></p></div>
 <h4>Design Practice</h4><div class="caPractice"><p>Design an AIM™ control plane for an IT response agent that may restart approved services autonomously but may not disable security controls, rotate privileged credentials, or make production firewall changes without authorization.</p><ol><li>Define the structured proposal sent by the agent.</li><li>Identify the policy decision point and the rules it evaluates.</li><li>Classify restart, firewall change, credential rotation, and security-control disablement using A/G/H/X.</li><li>Define the controlled adapters and their least-privilege identities.</li><li>Specify fail-closed behavior when authorization is unavailable.</li><li>Define evidence records and the external Last Command path.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="caCommandCheck"><b>ARCHITECT'S CHECK</b><p>Could the agent reach the consequential production capability without passing through the authorization layer? If yes, the control plane is not yet the control plane.</p></div>
 <div class="caAssessmentLaunch"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">ACA-102 ASSESSMENT</span><h4>Demonstrate Control-Plane Engineering Competency</h4><p>10 scored questions · 80% (8/10) required · retake available · badge earned only on pass.</p></div><button class="primary" onclick="showAca102Assessment()">TAKE ASSESSMENT →</button></div>
 </div>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
