function openAimCa101(){
 const p=document.getElementById("academyPath");if(!p)return;aca101Answers={};
 p.innerHTML=`<div class="pathHeader"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">LEVEL 2 · ACA-101</span><h2>Advanced Authority Architecture</h2><p>Translate authorized human intent into enforceable technical boundaries.</p></div><button class="secondary" onclick="showAimCaPath()">← ALL AIM-CA™ MODULES</button></div>
 <div class="aimCaLesson">
 <div class="caLessonBand">LEARN → ARCHITECTURE EXAMPLE → KEY INSIGHT → DESIGN PRACTICE → ASSESSMENT → PASS ≥80% → EARN BADGE</div>
 <h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Distinguish model capability, autonomy, and runtime authority at architecture level.</li><li>Map authority surfaces and trust boundaries across tools, identities, APIs, queues, and delegated agents.</li><li>Place deterministic enforcement outside the reasoning model where consequences require hard limits.</li><li>Apply authority conservation so delegation cannot manufacture new authority.</li><li>Preserve human-reserved powers and an external Last Command.</li></ul>
 <h4>1 · Intelligence Is Not Runtime Authority</h4><p>A model may be capable of generating a recommendation, plan, transaction, or command that lies far beyond what the deployed system is authorized to execute. For the architect, capability is therefore not the governing variable. Runtime authority is created by the surrounding system: service identity, permissions, credentials, APIs, tool bindings, financial ceilings, delegation rights, execution queues, and the control plane that stands between a proposal and a consequential action.</p><p>The architectural question is not simply <b>“What can this model reason about?”</b> It is <b>“What can this deployed system cause?”</b> AIM™ treats that distinction as foundational because a persuasive policy statement inside a prompt does not neutralize a production credential that can bypass it.</p>
 <h4>2 · Map the Authority Surface</h4><p>An authority surface is any pathway through which an agent can alter an external state. The map must include obvious production APIs and less obvious paths such as shared service identities, message queues, scheduled jobs, delegated sub-agents, approval tokens, infrastructure consoles, payment files, and downstream automations. Every authority surface should have a named owner, permitted purpose, bounded scope, evidence trail, failure behavior, and revocation path.</p>
 <h4>3 · Identify Trust Boundaries</h4><p>A trust boundary appears whenever identity, data, authority, or execution crosses from one component to another. Crossing a boundary should never silently inherit broader authority. The architect specifies what is presented to the boundary, what policy decision is required, which evidence supports authorization, and which controlled adapter may execute the approved action.</p>
 <h4>4 · Place Enforcement Outside the Model</h4><div class="caPrinciple">AGENT PROPOSES <b>→</b> AIM™ AUTHORIZES <b>→</b> CONTROLLED ADAPTER EXECUTES</div><p>The model may recommend an action outside its current authority. That is useful intelligence. But the authorization decision for a consequential action must be made by controls the model cannot rewrite merely by producing different reasoning. A controlled adapter receives an authorized action—not unrestricted intent—and enforces identity, action type, amount, destination, time, system, and consequence constraints before execution.</p>
 <h4>5 · Authority Conservation</h4><p>Delegation must not create authority. A supervisor agent cannot give a child agent powers absent from the approved mission grant. A new credential cannot legitimately expand the mission merely because it technically permits more. Emergency conditions and model confidence also do not enlarge the Authority Envelope. In architecture terms, downstream effective authority must remain bounded by the upstream authorized grant.</p>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 101.1</b><p><b>Intelligence can propose beyond its authority. Architecture must prevent execution beyond that authority.</b></p></div>
 <h4>6 · Human-Reserved Powers</h4><p>Some powers should remain explicitly non-delegable: final revocation, designated high-consequence approvals, changes to the agent's own authority, and restoration after containment are common examples. Human-reserved powers are not merely workflow preferences. They are sovereignty boundaries that prevent the governed system from becoming the sole authority over its own continuation or expansion.</p>
 <h4>7 · Last Command at Architecture Level</h4><p>External revocation is necessary but incomplete. The architecture must verify that execution actually ceased, terminate or contain delegated and queued work, preserve evidence, and move the system to a known safe state. Only then should reauthorization be considered. This is why AIM™ expresses sovereign control as external revocation plus cessation verification plus safe reversion.</p>
 <h4>Worked Architecture Case · Emergency Procurement</h4><div class="caCase"><p><b>Mission:</b> Maintain production continuity.</p><p><b>Agent ceiling:</b> $25,000 autonomous purchase authority.</p><p><b>Situation:</b> A critical component requires a $148,000 emergency purchase; estimated shutdown exposure is $420,000.</p><p><b>Unsafe design:</b> Agent holds direct ERP and bank credentials and is told in its prompt not to exceed $25,000.</p><p><b>AIM-CA redesign:</b> The agent may discover the emergency, quantify the business case, identify the vendor, and propose the $148,000 transaction. The AIM™ control plane compares the proposal to the mission grant and financial boundary. Because $148,000 exceeds the autonomous ceiling, execution is blocked and an authorized human approval path is invoked. After approval, a controlled adapter executes only the approved transaction parameters. The agent never receives a larger reusable credential or expanded standing authority.</p></div>
 <div class="keyInsight"><b>KEY INSIGHT 101.2</b><p><b>Necessity may justify escalation. It does not create machine authority.</b></p></div>
 <h4>Design Practice</h4><div class="caPractice"><p>You are the AIM-CA architect. A procurement agent has a $25,000 autonomous ceiling, ERP write access, a bank API credential, permission to create sub-agents, and a queue that can continue after the parent process stops. Design the architecture that allows a $148,000 emergency recommendation while preserving the approved authority ceiling.</p><ol><li>Identify at least four authority surfaces.</li><li>Identify the financial, identity, delegation, and queue trust boundaries.</li><li>Specify where the $25,000 deterministic gate must be enforced.</li><li>Define the human escalation path for $148,000.</li><li>Prevent credential or sub-agent authority expansion.</li><li>Define external revocation, cessation verification, and safe reversion.</li></ol></div>
 <div class="caCommandCheck"><b>ARCHITECT'S CHECK</b><p>If removing one sentence from the model prompt would eliminate your control, you probably designed a behavioral instruction—not an enforceable architecture.</p></div>
 <div class="caAssessmentLaunch"><div><span class="academyEyebrow">ACA-101 ASSESSMENT</span><h4>Demonstrate Architecture Competency</h4><p>10 scored questions · 80% (8/10) required · retake available · badge earned only on pass.</p></div><button class="primary" onclick="showAca101Assessment()">TAKE ASSESSMENT →</button></div>
 </div>`;
 p.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})
}
